<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bec Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1034</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bec Abbey, at Le Bec-Hellouin in Normandy, is a Benedictine monastery founded around 1034 by the knight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herluin">Herluin</a>. Under the teachers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanfranc">Lanfranc</a> and Anselm, both later archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, its school became one of the leading centres of learning in 11th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> it held estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and supplied many English abbots and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bec-abbey.jpg" alt="Bec Abbey" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: drawing by M.J. Ratel after a seal by an anonymous artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_Abbaye_du_Bec.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
